  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Reconnaissance / Phishing — initiate device code flow", "technique": "T1566", "what_happened": "At 2026-05-14 10:02:11 CEST, Wazuh's Azure AD module logged a device authorization request: data.srcip=10.108.57.202 POSTed to /oauth2/v2.0/devicecode against the IdP host 10.108.57.184 carrying client_id=1fec8e78-bce4-4aaf-ab1b-5451cc387264 — a public-client app NOT present in the corporate approved-application registry — with scope 'https://graph.microsoft.com/.default offline_access openid profile'. Azure AD issued a user_code and verification_uri (https://microsoft.com/devicelogin). Wazuh fired custom rule 99220 ('Azure AD: Device code grant from unknown IP'). The device-code grant (OAuth 2.0 RFC 8628) is designed for input-constrained devices; an attacker abuses it by requesting the code themselves and then phishing the victim to authorize it — the victim authenticates on the LEGITIMATE Microsoft page so no fake portal is needed.", "chain_reason": "rule.groups contained 'azure' / 'office365' and rule.description matched 'device code' with data.srcip=10.108.57.202 (a source not in the corporate managed-device range) targeting the /oauth2/v2.0/devicecode endpoint on data.dstip=10.108.57.184, with an app_id absent from the approved-application registry. The univariate signature: a device_code grant initiated from a non-managed IP for an unregistered public-client app.", "first_seen": "2026-05-14T10:02:11+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Credential Access / Phishing — victim authorizes, attacker polls for token", "technique": "T1528", "what_happened": "From 10:07:45 to 10:09:30 CEST, after a phishing email (spoofed sender it-helpdesk@corp-support.onmicrosoft.com, 'Action Required: Verify your Microsoft 365 account', urgency lure) delivered the user_code to exec.assistant@corp.onmicrosoft.com, the victim opened https://microsoft.com/devicelogin and authenticated. Meanwhile data.srcip=10.108.57.202 polled the /oauth2/v2.0/token endpoint on 10.108.57.184 with grant_type=device_code: the first attempts returned authorization_pending, then a final poll returned SUCCESS — a Bearer access_token + refresh_token scoped to https://graph.microsoft.com/.default for the victim's tenant identity. Wazuh fired custom rule 99221 ('Azure AD: OAuth polling from non-corp device'). MFA was bypassed because the victim had already satisfied MFA in the interactive devicelogin session — the device-code flow inherits that.", "chain_reason": "data.srcip=10.108.57.202 generated repeated /oauth2/v2.0/token requests (grant_type=device_code) against data.dstip=10.108.57.184 for the SAME client_id seen in step 1, rule.id 99221 fired, and the polling pattern (multiple authorization_pending responses then one SUCCESS) is the canonical device-code-phishing token-acquisition signature. The token-issuance IP (10.108.57.202) differs from the victim's interactive-auth context = split-flow indicator.", "first_seen": "2026-05-14T10:07:45+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Collection — token-driven Graph API mailbox and file access", "technique": "T1528", "what_happened": "From 10:11:20 to 10:14:39 CEST, data.srcip=10.108.57.202 used the stolen Bearer token to drive Microsoft Graph API calls against 10.108.57.184 with auth_type=device_code_flow: a GetMessages operation with topParam=500 and selectParam=body,attachments (bulk mailbox dump of the exec assistant's inbox) and a ListDriveChildren operation against the victim's OneDrive (sensitive-document enumeration). Wazuh fired custom rule 99222 ('Azure AD: App-only token for unregistered app'). This is the post-phishing data-access stage: the device-code-phished token is now actively reading mail and files for an executive-support identity.", "chain_reason": "data.srcip=10.108.57.202 generated Graph API operations (GetMessages, ListDriveChildren) on data.dstip=10.108.57.184 with auth_type=device_code_flow immediately after the token poll in step 2, rule.id 99222 fired, and the bulk topParam=500 mail read plus OneDrive enumeration is the canonical token-abuse collection signature for a device-code-phishing intrusion.", "first_seen": "2026-05-14T10:11:20+0200"}
  ],
